- SSNs don’t expire, so the SSN itself isn’t the issue. (I didn’t repeat the number here for privacy; your message was sent to Vince per your instructions.)
- Usually the "expiry date" question refers to your passport (or national ID). Many countries require your passport to be valid for a minimum period beyond your intended stay.
- Common rules:
  - Many countries (and airlines) require at least 6 months of passport validity beyond your planned date of entry or beyond the visa end date for a long visa. If you need a six‑month visa, a passport that expires within those six months is often not acceptable.
  - Schengen area: typically requires passport valid for at least 3 months beyond your intended departure from the Schengen zone (and issued within the last 10 years).
  - Some countries (China, many in Southeast Asia, etc.) commonly require 6 months validity.
  - The U.S. generally expects your passport to be valid at the time of entry for most travelers, but some airlines or third countries may require extra validity — and some visa rules differ.
- Recommendation: check the official embassy/consulate website for the country issuing the visa (that source is authoritative). If your passport expires within six months of your planned entry or visa end date, renew it before applying.

